Americano (cocktail):
{{Short description|Cocktail composed of Campari, sweet vermouth, and club soda}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=May 2023}}
{{Infobox drink
| name        = Americano
| image       = Americano cocktail at Nightwood Restaurant.jpg   
| type        = [[Mixed drink]]
| ingredients ={{plainlist|* 3 cl [[Campari]]
* 3 cl sweet red [[vermouth]]
* A splash of [[soda water]]}}
| served      = [[On the rocks]]: poured over ice
| garnish     = half an [[Orange (fruit)|orange]] slice, [[lemon]] twist
| prep        = Pour the Campari and vermouth over ice into an old fashioned glass, add a splash of soda water and garnish with half an orange slice and a lemon twist.
| drinkware   = [[Old fashioned glass]]
}}

The '''Americano''' is an [[List of IBA official cocktails|IBA official cocktail]]<ref name="IBA-wcc">{{cite web|url=http://iba-world.com/iba-cocktails/|title=The Unforgettables|work=[[International Bartenders Association]]|access-date=26 January 2016|archive-date=9 March 2017|archive-url=https://archive.today/20170309093042/http://iba-world.com/iba-cocktails/|url-status=dead}}</ref> composed of [[Campari]], [[sweet vermouth]], and for the sparkling version, [[club soda]] and garnished with a slice of lemon or an orange twist.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.tasteofhome.com/collection/2-ingredient-cocktails/|title = 2-Ingredient Cocktails You'll Want to Memorize}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Classics You Should Know: The Americano |url=https://www.liquor.com/recipes/americano/ |access-date=2025-01-06 |website=Liquor.com |language=en}}</ref>

== History ==
{{More citations needed section|date=August 2022}}
The cocktail was first served in creator [[Gaspare Campari]]'s [[Bar (establishment)|bar]], Caffè Campari in [[Milan]]. In the 1860s, an [[Americans|American]] man, who was under the impression that Campari was a [[long drink]], ordered it, hated it, and said it would be better served iced and [[fizzy]]. He ordered a Campari and [[Soda Water|soda]] which became too [[Taste|bitter]]; after a few iterations he and the esteemed bartender decided on [[Vermouth]] as the perfect blend.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Campari Takes Center Stage In This Tasty Americano Cocktail |url=https://www.thespruceeats.com/americano-cocktail-recipe-759279 |access-date=2024-11-05 |website=The Spruce Eats |language=en}}</ref> It is the direct descendant of the "Milano-Torino" which consisted of Campari, the bitter [[liqueur]] from [[Milan]] (Milano) and [[Punt e Mes]], the vermouth from [[Turin]] (Torino) but lacked soda water. This drink was itself a descendant of the "Torino-Milano", a concoction consisting of equal parts Campari and Amaro Cora.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2020-05-11 |title=The Americano and the Milano Torino cocktails - an introduction and instructions |url=https://omwines.com/cocktails/2020/5/11/the-americano-and-the-milano-torino-cocktails-an-introduction |access-date=2024-11-06 |website=OLIVER McCRUM WINES & SPIRITS |language=en-US}}</ref>

==In popular culture==
It is the first drink ordered by [[James Bond]] in the [[debut novel|first novel]] in [[Ian Fleming]]'s series, ''[[Casino Royale (novel)|Casino Royale]]''. In ''[[From Russia, with Love (novel)|From Russia With Love]]'', Bond drinks "two excellent Americanos" in [[Rome]] during his flight to [[Istanbul]]. In the [[short story]] "[[From a View to a Kill]]", Bond chooses an Americano as an appropriate drink for a mere café; suggesting that "in cafés you have to drink the least offensive of the musical comedy drinks that go with them."<ref>Fleming, Ian. "From a View to a Kill" in ''The Complete James Bond Short Stories''. New York, 2004.</ref> Bond always stipulates [[Perrier]], for, in his opinion, expensive soda water was the cheapest way to improve a poor drink. 

In ''[[The Tourist (2010 film)|The Tourist]]'', Elise and Fred, each had an Americano (or two) before their fancy dinner at a [[Venice|Venetian]] restaurant, and resumed drinking that post-dinner back in their hotel room. 

In ''[[The Talented Mr. Ripley]]'', Tom and Dickie drink americanos on the [[Via Veneto]] in Rome.

