Boulevardier (cocktail):
{{Short description|1920s cocktail of whiskey, sweet vermouth, and Campari}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=April 2016}}
{{Infobox drink
| name        = Boulevardier
| image       = VTR Happy Hour - Boulevardier.jpg   
| type        = [[List of cocktails|Cocktail]]
| ingredients ={{plainlist|* 45&nbsp;ml [[bourbon whiskey|bourbon]] or [[rye whiskey]]
* 30&nbsp;ml bitter [[Campari]]
* 30&nbsp;ml sweet red [[vermouth]]}}
| served      = [[Straight up]]: chilled, without ice
| garnish     = [[Orange zest]], optionally a lemon zest
| prep        = Pour all ingredients into mixing glass with ice cubes. Stir well. Strain into a chilled cocktail glass.
| drinkware   = [[Cocktail glass]]
}}

The '''boulevardier''' [[cocktail]] is an alcoholic drink composed of [[whiskey]], sweet [[vermouth]], and [[Campari]].<ref name=simonson>{{cite news|last1=Simonson|first1=Robert|author-link1=Robert Simonson|title=The Boulevardier Is Back on the Menu|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/29/dining/the-boulevardier-is-back-on-the-menu.html|access-date=19 April 2016|work=[[The New York Times]]|date=28 January 2014|archive-date=23 July 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210723190031/https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/29/dining/the-boulevardier-is-back-on-the-menu.html|url-status=live}}</ref> It originated as an obscure cocktail in late 1920s Paris, and was largely forgotten for 80 years, before being rediscovered in the late 2000s as part of the [[craft cocktail movement]], rapidly rising in popularity in the 2010s as a variant of the [[negroni]], and becoming an [[IBA official cocktail]] in 2020.

==History==
The boulevardier first appeared in print in the Parisian cocktail book ''Barflies and Cocktails'' (1927), where it is ascribed to [[Erskine Gwynne]], an American-born writer who founded a monthly magazine in Paris called ''Boulevardier'', which appeared from 1927 to 1932.<ref name=simonson/><ref>{{cite news| title=Erskine Gwynne, 49, Wrote Book on Paris|url=https://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1948/05/06/85217909.pdf |access-date=19 April 2016 |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=6 May 1948}}</ref>{{efn|Upon launching the magazine, which was apparently both humorous and literary, Gwynne said it would be "fast but clean".<ref>{{cite news|last1=James|first1=Edwin L.|title=Events of the Week Stir French Pride| url=https://timesmachine.nytimes.com/timesmachine/1927/02/20/98413090.pdf|access-date=19 April 2016|work=New York Times| date=20 February 1927}}</ref> The magazine published advertisements seeking subscribers in both ''Harry's ABC of Mixing Cocktails'' (1919, revised annually) and ''Barflies and Cocktails'' (1927, p. 111), the memoir and recipe collections of [[Harry MacElhone]] of Harry's New York Bar in Paris. MacElhone credited Gwynne, one of his regular customers, with inventing the drink.<ref name="Cecchini">{{cite news|date=2 February 2012|url=http://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/02/02/case-study-the-boulevardier/|title=Case Study: The Boulevardier|work=[[T (magazine)|T]]|access-date=7 November 2015|first=Toby|last=Cecchini|archive-date=6 September 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150906082928/http://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/02/02/case-study-the-boulevardier/|url-status=live}}</ref>}} The cocktail appears, not in the main list of recipes, but in the essay "Cocktails About Town" by [[Arthur Moss]], which describes cocktails by men-about-town. The boulevardier is described as an equal parts cocktail:{{sfn|MacElhone|1927|page=80}}
:Now is the time for all good Barflies to come to the aid of the party, since Erskinne [sic] Gwynne crashed in with his Boulevardier Cocktail; 1/3 Campari, 1/3 Italian vermouth, 1/3 Bourbon whisky.

The very similar "[[Old pal (cocktail)|Old Pal]]" cocktail appears in the same essay, differing only in using Canadian Club whisky, rather than bourbon.{{sfn|MacElhone|1927|page=82}}

The boulevardier did not catch on: no references to it have been found to it in cocktail books between 1927 and 2007, and it is absent from major collections.<ref name="armin">{{cite web |url=https://bar-vademecum.eu/boulevardier/ |title=Boulevardier |date=2017-05-14 |author=Armin |work=Bar-Vademecum |access-date=20 November 2023 |archive-date=12 June 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210612210537/https://bar-vademecum.eu/boulevardier/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="felten">{{cite news |url=https://www.washingtonexaminer.com/opinion/mysteries-of-the-boulevardier-and-negroni-solved |title=Mysteries of the boulevardier and Negroni, solved |first=Eric |last=Felten |date=2022-04-21 |work=[[Washington Examiner]] |archive-date=20 November 2023 |access-date=20 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231120055555/https://www.washingtonexaminer.com/opinion/mysteries-of-the-boulevardier-and-negroni-solved |url-status=live }}</ref> This contrasts with the Old pal, which appeared in the main list of recipes in later editions of MacElhone, such as {{harvtxt|MacElhone|1952}}, and was well-known enough to be one of the 50 cocktails in the inaugural 1961 list of [[IBA official cocktail]]s. This also contrasts with the negroni, which only appeared in the late 1940s, but became popular in the 1950s<ref name="felten" /> and also appeared in the 1961 IBA list.

The cocktail was rediscovered and popularized first by Ted Haigh, later by others,<ref name="armin" /><ref name="felten" /> starting with an article in the 2007 March/April issue of ''Imbibe'', writing as "Dr. Cocktail".<ref name="clarke2007">{{cite web |url=http://www.cocktailchronicles.com/2007/10/04/boulevardier/ |title=Boulevardier |date=2007-10-04 |author=Paul Clarke |work=The Cocktail Chronicles |access-date=20 November 2023 |archive-date=21 October 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071021040356/http://www.cocktailchronicles.com/2007/10/04/boulevardier/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://drbamboo.blogspot.com/2008/02/boulevardier-1-oz.html |title=Kinda like a Negroni...kinda like a Manhattan... |date=2008-02-29 |work=Dr. Bamboo |access-date=20 November 2023 |archive-date=19 August 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110819084506/http://drbamboo.blogspot.com/2008/02/boulevardier-1-oz.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Haigh subsequently published a recipe in {{harvtxt|Haigh|2009a}},{{sfn|Haigh|2009a|pages=[https://books.google.com/books?id=sCR7wWhM7IQC&q=boulevardier 75–76]}} together with further articles.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://imbibemagazine.com/the-history-of-the-boulevardier-cocktail/ |title=History Lesson: The Boulevardier |first=Ted |last=Haigh |work=Imbibe |date=2009-12-04 |access-date=20 November 2023 |archive-date=30 March 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100330034221/https://imbibemagazine.com/the-history-of-the-boulevardier-cocktail/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The cocktail was further popularized by Paul Clarke, who learned about it from Haigh,<ref name="clarke2007" /> and then published recipes on [[Serious Eats]]<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.seriouseats.com/boulevardier-recipe |title=Boulevardier |first=Paul |last=Clarke |orig-date=2008-09 |work=[[Serious Eats]] |access-date=20 November 2023 |archive-date=11 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210511103532/https://www.seriouseats.com/boulevardier-recipe |url-status=live }}</ref> and {{harvtxt|Clarke|2015}}. The boulevardier was still rare in the early 2010s,<ref name="Cecchini" /> but by the mid-2010s was popular.<ref name="simonson" />

The boulevardier was listed as an [[IBA official cocktail]] starting in the 2020 list, in the "Unforgettables" section.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://wildandwanderfultravel.com/index.php/2020/04/16/2020-iba-cocktail-list/ |title=The New 2020 IBA Cocktail List. The Quest (modified) |author=Jacquelyn |date=2020-04-16 |work=Wild and Wanderful Travel |access-date=19 November 2023 |archive-date=15 December 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201215220411/https://wildandwanderfultravel.com/index.php/2020/04/16/2020-iba-cocktail-list/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Similar drinks==
The [[Old pal (cocktail)|Old pal]] is a similar contemporary cocktail, appearing in the same essay in {{harvtxt|MacElhone|1927}}, originally differentiated by using rye whiskey ([[Canadian Club]]) instead of bourbon (otherwise identical equal parts cocktails with Italian vermouth and Campari). It is distinguished primarily by using dry "French" vermouth instead of sweet "Italian" vermouth; the IBA recipes also differ as equal parts (1:1:1) instead of the boulevardier's 1½:1:1 (3:2:2) and the whiskey always being rye, instead of bourbon or rye.

The boulevardier is similar to a [[Negroni]], sharing two of its three ingredients. It is differentiated primarily by its use of [[bourbon whiskey]] or [[rye whiskey]] as its principal component instead of gin.<ref name="Imbibe"/><ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.seriouseats.com/recipes/2008/09/boulevardier-recipe.html | title=Boulevardier | first=Paul | last=Clarke | access-date=19 April 2016 | work=[[Serious Eats]] | archive-date=14 September 2008 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080914092451/http://www.seriouseats.com/recipes/2008/09/boulevardier-recipe.html | url-status=live }}</ref> In the IBA standard recipes it is also distinguished by being served [[straight up]] (no ice, stemmed glass) instead of down on the rocks (ice, tumbler); having more spirit than other components (1½:1:1), instead of having equal parts; and being garnished with an orange twist, rather than an orange slice. All of these other than whiskey vs. gin vary in specific recipes, however.

==Variations==
Recipes vary the proportions of its components. The original 1927 recipe is equal parts (1:1:1), but Haigh's 2007 revival is 3:2:2, calling for {{sfrac|1|1|2}} parts rather than 1 part whiskey, and these are the proportions used in the IBA standard and many boulevardier recipes.<ref name="Imbibe">{{cite web| url=http://imbibemagazine.com/the-boulevardier-cocktail-recipe/| work=[[Imbibe Magazine]]| access-date=19 April 2016| title=Boulevardier Cocktail| archive-date=30 March 2010| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100330213000/http://imbibemagazine.com/the-boulevardier-cocktail-recipe/| url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Cecchini" /> Others are heavier still (2:1:1), and call for two parts bourbon to one part vermouth and one part Campari.<ref>{{cite news |last=Simonson |first=Robert |author-link=Robert Simonson |title=Boulevardier |url=http://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/1016009-boulevardier |work=[[The New York Times]] |access-date=19 April 2016 |archive-date=28 April 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160428173000/http://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/1016009-boulevardier |url-status=live }}</ref>

