| ingredients ={{plainlist|* 45 ml [[gin]]
* 45 ml [[orange juice]]
* 1 tablespoon [[absinthe]]
* 1 tablespoon [[grenadine]]}}
| served      = [[Straight up]]: chilled, without ice
| prep        = Pour all ingredients into a [[cocktail shaker]], shake well with ice, strain into a chilled cocktail glass.
| drinkware   = [[Cocktail glass]]
}}

The '''Monkey Gland''' is a [[cocktail]] of [[gin]], [[orange juice]], [[grenadine]] and [[absinthe]]  created in the 1920s by [[Harry MacElhone]], owner of [[Harry's New York Bar]] in [[Paris, France]].<ref name="fieldguide">{{cite book |first=Rob |last=Chirico |title=Field Guide to Cocktails |page=189 |year=2005 |publisher=Quirk Productions |isbn=1-59474-063-1}}</ref>

Some recipes substitute [[absinthe]] with [[pastis]] or [[Bénédictine]].<ref>{{cite book |last1=Haigh |first1=Ted |title=Vintage spirits and forgotten cocktails : from the alamagoozlum to the zombie and beyond : 100 rediscovered recipes and the stories behind them |date=2009 |publisher=Quarry Books |location=Beverly, Mass. |isbn=9781592535613 |pages=212–213 |edition=Deluxe; rev. & expanded|oclc=1023288091}}</ref>

The cocktail is known for its distinctive flavor, which combines the sweetness of orange with the herbal, slightly bitter notes of absinthe, creating a complex and intriguing profile.<ref>{{cite web |last=Valencourt |first=Aurelia |title=Monkey Gland |url=https://aureliavalencourt.com/cocktails/d369e916-c70d-4ded-acbd-eecbbc9a81d3/monkey-gland |website=AureliaValencourt |accessdate=February 28, 2025}}</ref>

It is  named after the pseudo-scientific idea that grafting [[monkey]] [[testicle]] tissue into humans would increase [[longevity]], the idea developed by the Russian doctor [[Serge Voronoff]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Hirst |first=Christopher |date=October 8, 2005 |work=[[The Independent]] |title=101 cocktails that shook the world #37: The Monkey Gland |department=Features |page=57}}</ref>

