Stinger (cocktail):
{{Short description|Cocktail made from crème de menthe and brandy}}
{{Infobox drink
| name        = Stinger
| image       = Stinger_Cocktail.jpg  
| caption     = Stinger cocktail served over ice in a rocks glass 
| type        = [[List of cocktails|Cocktail]]
| base        = [[Cognac (drink)|Cognac]]
| ingredients ={{plainlist|* 50&nbsp;ml [[cognac]]
* 20&nbsp;ml white [[crème de menthe]]}}
| served      = [[Straight up]]: chilled, without ice
| garnish     = Optional mint leaf
| prep        = Pour all ingredients into mixing glass with ice cubes. Stir well. Strain into chilled martini cocktail glass.
| drinkware   = [[Cocktail glass]]
}}

A '''stinger''' is a duo [[cocktail]] made by adding [[crème de menthe]] to [[brandy]] (although recipes vary). The cocktail's origins can be traced to the United States in the 1890s, and the beverage remained widely popular in America until the 1970s. It was seen as a drink of the [[upper class]], and has had a somewhat wide cultural impact.

==History of the cocktail==
The stinger originated about 1890.{{sfn|McCammon|Wondrich|2016|page=178}} The cocktail may have been derived from The Judge, a cocktail made with brandy, crème de menthe, and [[Syrup#Simple syrup|simple syrup]] found in William Schmidt's 1892 cocktail book ''The Flowing Bowl''.{{sfn|Regan|2003|page=24}} It was immediately popular in [[New York City]],{{sfn|Chirico|2005|page=262}} and quickly became known as a "[[High society (social class)|society]]" drink (i.e. only for the upper classes).{{sfn|Wondrich|2015|page=217}} According to bartender Jere Sullivan in his 1930 volume ''The Drinks of Yesteryear: A Mixology'', the stinger remained a critical component of the bartender's repertoire until [[Prohibition in the United States|Prohibition]].{{sfn|Regan|2003|page=26}}

The stinger was not initially seen as a cocktail (i.e., a drink served before dinner), but rather a [[Apéritif and digestif|digestif]] (after-dinner drink).{{sfn|Wondrich|2015|page=217}} Writing in the 1910s and 1920s, humorist [[Don Marquis]]'s "Hermione" (a fictional daffy society [[Busybody|do-gooder]]) refused to refer to the stinger as a cocktail, indicating its status in upper-class society. Over time, however, the stinger came to be consumed like a cocktail.{{sfn|Wondrich|2015|page=217}}

The stinger was a popular drink during [[Prohibition in the United States]], for crème de menthe could mask the taste of the inferior-quality brandies then available.{{sfn|Hellmich|2006|page=139}} The stinger began to lose favor with Americans in the late 1970s,{{sfn|DeGroff|2008|page=228}} and was not a well-known cocktail in the early 21st century.{{sfn|Rathbun|2007|page=43}}

==Recipe==
The stinger is a duo cocktail, in that it uses only two ingredients: a [[Distilled beverage|spirit]] and a [[liqueur]].{{sfn|Regan|2003|pages=141-142}} The classic stinger recipe uses three parts [[brandy]] and one part white crème de menthe.{{sfn|McCammon|Wondrich|2016|pages=178, 180}} However, stinger recipes vary, and some recipes call for equal parts brandy and crème de menthe.{{sfn|DeGroff|2008|page=228}} The mixture was originally stirred,{{sfn|Wondrich|2015|page=217}} although modern recipes call for it to be shaken with cracked ice.{{sfn|McCammon|Wondrich|2016|pages=178, 180}} Early recipes required that a stinger be served [[straight up]], but since the end of Prohibition in the United States{{sfn|Calabrese|2015|page=164}} it became more common for it to be served over crushed ice.{{sfn|Regan|2003|page=340}}

[[Cognac]], a type of brandy, was identified as the basis for the stinger as early as 1905 in [[William "Cocktail" Boothby]]'s supplement to his 1900 book, ''American Bar-Tender''.{{sfn|Wondrich|2015|page=217}} In the 21st century, cognac is the most commonly used brandy cited by recipes for the stinger's base liquor.{{sfn|Chirico|2005|page=262}}

Cocktail guides recommend that it be served in a cocktail glass if served straight,{{sfn|McCammon|Wondrich|2016|pages=178, 180}} or in a [[Old Fashioned glass|rocks]] glass if served with ice.{{sfn|Kingwell|2007|page=225}}

===Variations===
The '''amaretto stinger''' uses a 3-to-1 ratio of [[amaretto]] to white crème de menthe,{{sfn|''The Art of Mixology''|2015|page=62}} while an '''Irish stinger''' uses equal parts [[Irish cream|Irish cream liqueur]] and white crème de menthe.{{sfn|''The Art of Mixology''|2015|page=63}}

The '''Mexican stinger''' substitutes [[tequila]] for brandy.{{sfn|Shaw|2008|page=134}}

A "vodka stinger", also known as a '''white spider''',{{sfn|DeGroff|2008|page=229}} uses vodka instead of brandy.{{sfn|Kingwell|2007|pages=190-191}}

The '''white way cocktail''', which celebrates [[Broadway theatre]], is a stinger made with [[gin]] rather than brandy.{{sfn|Kingwell|2007|page=190}}

Mixologists Oliver Said and James Mellgren cite a cocktail known as the stinger sour. It is made with a 3-to-1-to-1 ratio of [[bourbon whiskey|bourbon]], [[peppermint schnapps]], and lemon juice.{{sfn|Said|Mellgren|2005|page=177}} This cocktail is not technically a stinger, since it omits the crème de menthe.{{sfn|Hellmich|2006|page=139}}{{sfn|Rombauer|Becker|Becker|2002|page=120}}{{sfn|Walker|1980|page=51}}

==Cultural impact==
The stinger's popularity in New York City was so great that [[urban legend]]s attributed the cocktail's genesis to famous millionaire [[Reginald Claypoole Vanderbilt|Reginald Vanderbilt]]. It was further claimed that the stinger was Vanderbilt's favorite cocktail, and he spent hours making them for his guests.{{sfn|Wondrich|2015|page=217}} He would shake them up for himself and friends in his barroom, always with a dash of absinthe added. But Vanderbilt did not call this drink the Brant or the Bartholdi—he called it the Stinger.<ref>{{cite magazine|work=Punch|author=CULLITON|title=New York Has Always Been a Stinger Town|year=2022}}</ref>

The stinger's reputation as a high-society drink led to its appearance in several famous novels. James Bond and Tiffany Case each have a stinger in the 1956 [[Ian Fleming]] novel ''[[Diamonds Are Forever (novel)|Diamonds are Forever]]''.{{sfn|Holloway|2002|page=45}} The spy Alec Leamas drinks stingers in [[John le Carré]]'s 1963 novel ''[[The Spy Who Came in from the Cold]]''.{{sfn|Sandham|2012|page=39}}

The vodka stinger was the drink of choice for Joanne in the musical ''[[Company (musical)|Company]]'' by [[Stephen Sondheim]], with her calling for one in the song "[[The Ladies Who Lunch (song)|The Ladies Who Lunch]]".{{sfn|Kelly|2008|page=149}}

The drink is also featured in the 2007 ''[[Mad Men]]'' season one episode "[[Mad Men (season 1)|Nixon v. Kennedy]]", set in 1960. The episode features stingers made with [[Bacardi]] rum, as Bacardi was a series sponsor.{{sfn|Gelman|Zheutlin|2011|page=28}}

Women in [[Lawrence Block]] novels sometimes drink stingers. In ''The Burglar Who Dropped In On Elvis'' Bernie's date "had two dry Rob Roys first, most of the dinner wine, and a stinger afterward. I had a Bloody Mary for openers, and my after-dinner drink was a cup of coffee. I felt like a cheap date."<ref>{{Cite book|url=https://arounddate.com/lawrence-block-the-burglar-who-dropped-in-on-elvis/|author=Lawrence Block|title=The Burglar Who Dropped in on Elvis}}</ref> And in ''The Burglar in the Closet'', "Frankie's drinking, on the other hand, was certainly serious enough to keep the Recovery Room anchored in grim reality. A stinger is always a reasonably serious proposition. A brace of stingers at four o'clock on a weekday afternoon is about as serious as you can get."<ref>{{Cite book|url=https://silo.pub/the-burglar-in-the-closet-bernie-rhodenbarr-mysteries.html|title=The Burglar in the Closet|author=Lawrence Block}}</ref>

The stinger was widely mentioned in American motion pictures. Dudley the angel (played by Cary Grant) orders a round of stingers while lunching with ladies from the church in the 1947 film ''[[The Bishop's Wife]]''.{{sfn|Kingwell|2007|pages=61-62}} The evolving stinger (used with green rather than white crème de menthe) forms a plot point in the 1948 film ''[[The Big Clock (film)|The Big Clock]]'', when George Stroud ([[Ray Milland]]) orders one and a random woman in the bar ([[Rita Johnson]]) already knows his name.{{sfn|Kingwell|2007|page=62}}  In the 1950 [[Humphrey Bogart]] and [[Gloria Grahame]] film ''[[In a Lonely Place]]'', Dix Steele's lush friend Charlie orders a stinger at Paul's bar before the first fight scene. In the 1956 [[Bing Crosby]] and [[Frank Sinatra]] film ''[[High Society (1956 film)|High Society]]'', Dexter-Haven's butler offers Stingers at lunch to those who over-indulged in champagne during the previous evening's party.{{sfn|Kingwell|2007|page=60}} Cary Grant again orders stingers ("and keep them coming") as he tries to tolerate character Alice Kratzner's ([[Jayne Mansfield]]) empty-headed babbling in the 1957 comedy film ''[[Kiss Them for Me (film)|Kiss Them for Me]]''.{{sfn|Wohl|2012|page=45}} Mr. Dobitsch ([[Ray Walston]]) instructs his [[Marilyn Monroe]] look-alike date ([[Joyce Jameson]]) in the 1960 film ''[[The Apartment]]'' to not spill the glasses of stingers she is holding as they exit their cab and enter C.C. Baxter's ([[Jack Lemmon]]) apartment at night for a tryst. In the 1975 [[Warren Beatty]] film ''[[Shampoo (film)|Shampoo]]'', at the Republican Party dinner scene [[Goldie Hawn]] says, "I'll have a stinger", to which [[Tony Bill]] replies, "Before dinner?"{{sfn|Kingwell|2007|page=61}}

