Pisco sour:
{{Short description|Cocktail in Chilean and Peruvian cuisine}}
{{featured article}}
{{Infobox drink
| name        = Pisco sour
| image       = Pisco sour 20100613b.JPG 
| image_alt   = Photograph 
| caption     = Peruvian pisco sour 
| type        = [[List of cocktails|Cocktail]]
| base        = [[Pisco]]
| ingredients ={{plainlist|* 60&nbsp;ml [[pisco]]
* 30&nbsp;ml fresh lemon juice
* 20&nbsp;ml [[simple syrup]]
* 1 raw [[egg white]]}}
| served      = [[Straight up]]: chilled, without ice
| garnish     = Few dashes of [[Amargo Chuncho|Amargo bitters]] on top as an aromatic garnish
| prep        = Add all ingredients into a shaker with ice. Shake and strain into a chilled goblet glass.
| drinkware   = [[Goblet]]
}}

A '''pisco sour''' is an alcoholic [[cocktail]] of [[Peru]]vian origin that is traditional to [[Peruvian cuisine|Peruvian cuisine]]. The drink's name comes from ''[[pisco]]'', a brandy which is its base liquor, and the cocktail term ''[[Sour (cocktail)|sour]]'', implying sour citrus juice and sweetener components. The Peruvian pisco sour uses Peruvian ''pisco'' and adds freshly squeezed lime juice, [[simple syrup]], ice, [[egg white]], and [[Angostura bitters]]. The Chilean version is similar, but uses Chilean ''pisco'' and [[Limón de Pica|Pica lime]], and excludes the bitters and egg white. Other variants of the cocktail include those created with fruits like [[pineapple]] or plants such as [[coca]] leaves.

Although the preparation of pisco-based mixed beverages possibly dates back to the 1700s, historians and drink experts agree that the cocktail as it is known today was invented in the early 1920s in [[Lima]], the capital of Peru, by the American [[bartender]] [[Victor Vaughen Morris]].<ref name="Origin" /> Morris left the United States in 1903 to work in [[Cerro de Pasco]], a city in central Peru. In 1916, he opened Morris' Bar in Lima, and his saloon quickly became a popular spot for the Peruvian upper class and English-speaking foreigners. The oldest known mentions of the pisco sour are found in newspaper and magazine advertisements, dating to the early 1920s, for Morris and his bar published in Peru and Chile. The pisco sour underwent several changes until Mario Bruiget, a Peruvian bartender working at Morris' Bar, created the modern Peruvian recipe for the cocktail in the latter part of the 1920s by adding Angostura bitters and egg whites to the mix.

Cocktail connoisseurs consider the pisco sour a [[South American cuisine|South American]] classic.{{efn-ua|Despite the pisco sour being predominantly emblematic along the [[Pacific Ocean|Pacific coast]] of Peru and Chile, Casey calls it a "classic South American drink" and Bovis says it is "a hallmark South American cocktail."{{sfn|Casey|2009|p=89}}{{sfn|Bovis|2012|loc=Pisco Sour}}}} Chile and Peru both claim the pisco sour as their [[List of national liquors|national drink]], and each asserts ownership of the cocktail's base liquor—pisco;{{efn-ua|Peru considers that the name "pisco" should be of exclusive [[geographical indication]] to the aguardiente produced in designated regions within its territory.<ref name="Acosta Gonzalez">{{Cite web |last=Acosta González |first=Martín |date=22 May 2012 |title=Qué países reconocen el pisco como peruano y cuáles como chileno |url=http://elcomercio.pe/gastronomia/peruana/que-paises-reconocen-pisco-como-peruano-cuales-como-chileno-noticia-1418070 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170327190924/http://elcomercio.pe/gastronomia/peruana/que-paises-reconocen-pisco-como-peruano-cuales-como-chileno-noticia-1418070 |archive-date=27 March 2017 |access-date=5 October 2015 |website=El Comercio |publisher=Empresa Editora El Comercio |language=es}}</ref> Chile also has designated regions of pisco production within its territory, but claims exclusivity of the liquor under the name "Pisco Chile."<ref name="Acosta Gonzalez" />}} consequently, the pisco sour has become a significant and oft-debated topic of [[Latin America]]n popular culture. Media sources and celebrities commenting on the dispute often express their preference for one cocktail version over the other, sometimes just to cause controversy. Some pisco producers have noted that the controversy helps promote interest in the drink. The two kinds of pisco and the two variations in the style of preparing the pisco sour are distinct in both production and taste. Peru celebrates yearly in honor of the cocktail on the first Saturday of February.

== Name ==
The term ''sour'' refers to mixed drinks containing a base [[liquor]], lemon or lime juice, and a sweetener.{{sfn|Regan|2003|p=158}} ''Pisco'' refers to the base liquor used in the cocktail. The word as applied to the alcoholic beverage comes from the Peruvian port of [[Pisco, Peru|Pisco]]. In the book ''Latin America and the Caribbean'', historian Olwyn Blouet and political geographer Brian Blouet describe the development of vineyards in early [[Viceroyalty of Peru|Colonial Peru]] and how in the second half of the sixteenth century a market for the liquor formed owing to the demand from growing mining settlements in the [[Andes]]. Subsequent demand for a stronger drink caused Pisco and the nearby city of [[Ica, Peru|Ica]] to establish distilleries "to make wine into brandy",{{sfn|Blouet|Blouet|2009|p=318}} and the product received the name of the port from where it was distilled and exported.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Pisco |url=http://lema.rae.es/drae/?val=pisco |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150704235922/http://lema.rae.es/drae/?val=pisco |archive-date=4 July 2015 |access-date=3 July 2015 |website=Diccionario de la Lengua Española |language=es |edition=Vigésima Segunda Edición}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Pisco |url=http://www.wordreference.com/definition/pisco |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150705061106/http://www.wordreference.com/definition/pisco |archive-date=5 July 2015 |access-date=3 July 2015 |website=Concise Oxford Dictionary |publisher=WordReference.com}}</ref>

== History ==
=== Background ===
{{See also|New World wine}}
[[File:Plaza Acho.jpg|thumb|right|alt= Aerial photograph| The [[Plaza de toros de Acho]] (Lima), the oldest [[bullring]] in the Americas, where the predecessor to the pisco sour was sold]]

The first [[Vitis|grapevines]] were brought to Peru shortly after its [[Spanish conquest of the Inca Empire|conquest by Spain]] in the 16th century. Spanish [[chronicler]]s from the time note the first winemaking in South America took place in the ''[[hacienda]]'' Marcahuasi of [[Cusco|Cuzco]].{{sfn|Franco|1991|p=20}} The largest and most prominent vineyards of the 16th and 17th century Americas were established in the Ica valley of south-central Peru.<ref name="disp">{{Cite web |last=Harrel |first=Courtney |year=2009 |title=Pisco Por La Razón o La Fuerza |url=http://digitalcollections.sit.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1712&context=isp_collection&sei-redir=1&referer=http%3A%2F%2Fscholar.google.com%2Fscholar%3Fstart%3D10%26q%3Dpisco%2Baguardiente%26hl%3Des%26as_sdt%3D0%2C5#search=%22pisco%20aguardiente%22 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170202141731/http://digitalcollections.sit.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1712&context=isp_collection&sei-redir=1&referer=http%3A%2F%2Fscholar.google.com%2Fscholar%3Fstart%3D10%26q%3Dpisco%2Baguardiente%26hl%3Des%26as_sdt%3D0%2C5#search=%22pisco%20aguardiente%22 |archive-date=2017-02-02 |access-date=2012-04-01 |publisher=School for International Training |pages=14–15 |language=es}}</ref><ref name=Lacoste /> In the 1540s, Bartolomé de Terrazas and Francisco de Carabantes planted vineyards in Peru.{{sfn|Pozo|2004|pp=24–34}} Carabantes also established vineyards in Ica, where Spaniards from [[Andalusia|Andalucia]] and [[Extremadura]] introduced grapevines into Chile.{{sfn|Pozo|2004|pp=24–34}}<ref name="Olivares" />

Already in the 16th century, Spanish settlers in Chile and Peru began producing [[aguardiente]]<ref name="Olivares">{{Cite journal |last=Cortés Olivares |first=Hernán F |year=2005 |title=El origen, producción y comercio del pisco chileno, 1546–1931 |trans-title=The origin, production and trade of Chilean pisco, 1546–1931 |journal=[[Revista Universum|Universum]] |language=es |volume=20 |issue=2 |pages=42–81 |doi=10.4067/S0718-23762005000200005 |doi-access=free}}</ref><ref name="Huertas">{{Cite journal |last=Huertas Vallejos |first=Lorenzo |year=2004 |title=Historia de la producción de vinos y piscos en el Perú |trans-title=History of the production of wine and pisco in Peru |journal=Universum |language=es |volume=19 |issue=2 |pages=44–61 |doi=10.4067/S0718-23762004000200004 |doi-access=free}}</ref> distilled from fermented grapes.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Aguardiente |url=http://lema.rae.es/drae/?val=aguardiente |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150705015721/http://lema.rae.es/drae/?val=aguardiente |archive-date=5 July 2015 |access-date=3 July 2015 |website=Diccionario de la Lengua Española |language=es |edition=Vigésima Segunda Edición}}</ref> Since at least 1764, Peruvian aguardiente was called "pisco" after its port of shipping;<ref name=disp /><ref name="Lacoste">{{Cite journal |last=Lacoste |first=Pablo |year=2004 |title=La vid y el vino en América del Sur: El desplazamiento de los polos vitivinícolas (siglos XVI al XX) |trans-title=The vine and wine in South America: The shifting of the winemaking poles (XVI to XX) |journal=Universum |language=es |volume=19 |issue=2 |pages=62–93 |doi=10.4067/S0718-23762004000200005 |doi-access=free}}</ref> the usage of the name "pisco" for aguardiente then spread to Chile.{{efn-ua|In [[colonial Chile]] the word "pisco" was mostly used by the lower social strata and is rarely found in the upper class speech of the epoch.<ref name=Olivares />}} The right to produce and market pisco, still made in Peru and Chile, is the subject of ongoing disputes between the two countries.{{sfn|Foley|2011|p="Pisco Porton Brandy Recipes"}}

According to historian [[Luciano Revoredo]], the preparation of pisco with lime dates as far back as the 18th century. He bases his claim on a source found in the ''[[Mercurio Peruano]]'' which details the prohibition of aguardiente in Lima's ''[[Plaza de toros de Acho]]'', the oldest bullring in the Americas. At this time, the drink was named ''Punche'' ([[Punch (drink)|Punch]]), and was sold by slaves. Revoredo further argues this drink served as the predecessor of the Californian [[pisco punch]], invented by Duncan Nicol in the Bank Exchange Bar of [[San Francisco]], California.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Pilar Lazo Rivera |first=Carmen del |year=2009 |title=Pisco Sour del Perú |pages=53–54 |url=http://www.pediatriaperu.org/images/stories/PDF/62N1/revista%20pediatria%20interior.pdf#page=59 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160303221227/http://www.pediatriaperu.org/images/stories/PDF/62N1/revista%20pediatria%20interior.pdf#page=59 |archive-date=3 March 2016 |access-date=3 July 2015 |publisher=Pediatraperu.org |language=es}}</ref> According to a 1921 news clip from the ''West Coast Leader'', an English-language newspaper from Peru, a saloon in San Francisco's [[Barbary Coast, San Francisco|Barbary Coast]] red-light district was known for its Pisco sours during "the old pre-[[Volstead Act|Volstead]] days".{{sfn|McDonnell|2015|p=171}} Culinary expert Duggan McDonnell considers that this attributes the popularity (not origin) of a pisco cocktail in San Francisco dating as far back as before the [[1906 San Francisco earthquake|1906 earthquake]] that destroyed the Barbary Coast.{{sfn|McDonnell|2015|p=171}} A recipe for a pisco-based punch, including egg whites, was found by researcher Nico Vera in the 1903 Peruvian cookbook ''Manual de Cocina a la Criolla''; consequently, McDonnell considers that "[i]t is entirely possible that the 'Cocktail' that came to be the pisco sour&nbsp;... had been prepared for a reasonable time in Lima before being included in a cookbook."{{sfn|McDonnell|2015|p=172}}

=== Origin ===
[[File:VVM Younger.jpg|thumb|right|upright=0.6|alt=Photograph of a man|Victor Morris, inventor of the pisco sour]]

The pisco sour originated in Lima, Peru.<ref name="Origin">See:
* {{harvnb|DeGroff|2008|loc=Pisco Sour}}
* {{harvnb|Duecy|2013|loc=Pisco Sour}}
* {{harvnb|Kosmas|Zaric|2010|p=115}}
* {{harvnb|Parsons|2011|p=143}}
* {{harvnb|Roque|2013|loc=Pisco Sour}}</ref> It was created by bartender Victor Vaughen Morris, an American from a respected [[Mormons|Mormon]] family of [[Welsh people|Welsh]] ancestry, who moved to Peru in 1904 to work in a railway company in [[Cerro de Pasco]].<ref name="Tatiana">{{Cite web |last=Perich |first=Tatiana |date=5 February 2015 |title=Les Presentamos a Mario Bruiget, el Peruano Coinventor del pisco sour |url=http://elcomercio.pe/gastronomia/bares-y-copas/les-presentamos-mario-bruiget-peruano-coinventor-pisco-sour-noticia-406073 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150703000916/http://elcomercio.pe/gastronomia/bares-y-copas/les-presentamos-mario-bruiget-peruano-coinventor-pisco-sour-noticia-406073 |archive-date=3 July 2015 |access-date=2 July 2015 |website=El Comercio |publisher=Empresa Editora El Comercio |language=es}}</ref><ref name="Baird">{{Cite web |last=Baird |first=Saxon |date=1 March 2018 |title=How a Mining Boom Led a Mormon Florist to Invent the Pisco Sour |url=https://www.atlasobscura.com/articles/pisco-sour-peru-inventor |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210116070601/https://www.atlasobscura.com/articles/pisco-sour-peru-inventor |archive-date=16 January 2021 |access-date=18 June 2021 |website=Atlas Obscura}}</ref> Americans emigrated to the bustling Andean mining hub of Cerro de Pasco, then the second-largest city in Peru, for work in the business ventures established by the tycoon [[Alfred W. McCune]].<ref name=Baird /> Morris, who worked as a [[Floristry|floral shop]] manager in [[Salt Lake City]], joined McCune's project to construct what was then the [[List of highest railways|highest-altitude railway in the world]] and ease the city's export of its [[precious metal]]s.<ref name=Baird /> During celebrations for the railway's completion in July 1904, Morris, tasked with overseeing the festivities, recalled first mixing pisco in a cocktail beverage after the nearly 5,000 American and Peruvian attendees (including local celebrities and dignitaries) consumed all of the available [[Whisky|whiskey]].<ref name=Baird />

Morris relocated to the Peruvian capital, Lima, with his Peruvian wife and three children in 1915. A year later, he opened a [[Bar (establishment)|saloon]]—Morris' Bar—which became popular with both the Peruvian upper class and English-speaking foreigners.<ref name=Tatiana /><ref name=Baird /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Karydes |first=Megy |date=11 December 2009 |title=Celebrate The Peruvian Cocktail On World Pisco Sour Day, Feb 7 |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/megykarydes/2015/02/05/celebrate-the-peruvian-cocktail-on-world-pisco-sour-day-feb-7/ |access-date=3 July 2015 |website=Forbes}}</ref> Morris, who often experimented with new drinks, developed the pisco sour as a variant of the whiskey sour.{{sfn|Kosmas|Zaric|2010|p=115}} Chilean historian [[Gonzalo Vial Correa]] also attributes the pisco sour's invention to ''Gringo'' Morris from the Peruvian Morris Bar, but with the minor difference of naming him William Morris.{{sfn|Vial Correa|1981|p=352}}

Some discrepancy exists on the exact date when Morris created the popular cocktail. Mixologist [[Dale DeGroff]] asserts the drink was invented in 1915,{{sfn|DeGroff|2008|loc=Pisco Sour}} but other sources argue this happened in the 1920s.<ref>See:
* {{harvnb|Kosmas|Zaric|2010|p=115}}
* {{harvnb|Parsons|2011|p=143}}</ref> The Chilean web newspaper ''[[El Mercurio]] Online'' specifically contends historians attribute the year of the drink's invention as 1922, adding that "one night Morris surprised his friends with a new drink he called ''pisco sour'', a formula which mixes the Peruvian ''pisco'' with the American ''[[Sour (cocktail)|sour]]''" (in Spanish: "''Una noche Morris sorprendió a sus amigos con una nueva bebida a la que llamó pisco sour, una fórmula que funde lo peruano del pisco con el 'sour' estadounidense.''").<ref name="Emol">{{Cite web |date=5 February 2011 |title=Peruanos Celebran el "Día del Pisco Sour" con Degustaciones y Fiestas |url=http://www.emol.com/noticias/economia/2011/02/05/462760/peruanos-celebran-el-dia-del-pisco-sour-con-degustaciones-y-fiestas.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150702201023/http://www.emol.com/noticias/economia/2011/02/05/462760/peruanos-celebran-el-dia-del-pisco-sour-con-degustaciones-y-fiestas.html |archive-date=2 July 2015 |access-date=3 July 2015 |publisher=Emol.com |language=es |agency=Agence France-Presse}}</ref>

[[File:Morris-Bar-Pisco-Sour-South-Pacific-Mail-1924.JPG|thumb|left|upright=0.35|alt=Snippet of an old newspaper|Advertisement for Morris' Bar and Register{{efn-ua|The image reads: "Have You Registered in Morris' Bar LIMA? You will find the names and addresses of many of your friends in this register. It is at the free disposition of all English-speaking persons who reside in or who pass through Lima. MORRIS' BAR at CALLE BOZA, 836, LIMA, Peru, has been noted for many years for its "pisco sours" and its reputation for "Legitimate Liquors." The Bar Register has become a veritable "Who's Who" among West Coast travellers and many friends have been located through the information within its pages."}}]]

The pisco sour's initial recipe was that of a simple cocktail.{{sfn|Duecy|2013|loc=Pisco Sour}} According to Peruvian researcher Guillermo Toro-Lira, "it is assumed that it was a crude mix of pisco with lime juice and sugar, as was the whiskey sour of those days."<ref name="Toro">{{Cite web |last=Toro-Lira |first=Guillermo L. |date=11 December 2009 |title=Clarifying the Legends from the History of the pisco sour |url=http://www.piscopunch.com/articles.php |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150221123508/http://piscopunch.com/articles.php |archive-date=21 February 2015 |access-date=2 July 2015 |publisher=Piscopunch.com}} ([https://wingsofcherubs.com/2020/04/18/clarifying-the-legends-from-the-history-of-the-pisco-sour/ A re-print and modern version of the article]; [https://web.archive.org/web/20210620123425/https://wingsofcherubs.com/clarifying-the-legends-from-the-history-of-the-pisco-sour/ archived] from the original on 20 June 2021)</ref> As the cocktail's recipe continued to evolve, the bar's registry shows that customers commented on the continuously improving taste of the drink.<ref name=Toro /> The modern Peruvian version of the recipe was developed by Mario Bruiget, a Peruvian from [[Chincha Alta]] who worked under the [[apprenticeship]] of Morris starting on July 16, 1924. Bruiget's recipe added the Angostura bitters and egg whites to the mix.<ref name=Tatiana /> Journalist Erica Duecy writes that Bruiget's innovation added "a silky texture and frothy head" to the cocktail.{{sfn|Duecy|2013|loc=Pisco Sour}}

Morris used advertisements to promote his bar and invention. The oldest known mention of the pisco sour appears in the September 1920 edition of the Peruvian magazine ''Hogar''.{{sfn|Jiménez Morato|2012|loc=Julio Ramon Ribeyro: Pisco Sour}} Another old advertisement appears in the April 22, 1921, edition of the Peruvian magazine ''Mundial''. In the magazine, not only is the pisco sour described as a white-colored beverage but its invention is attributed to "Mister Morris."<ref name="Coloma">{{Cite web |last=Coloma Porcari |first=César |year=2005 |title=La Verdadera Historia del Pisco Sour |url=https://sites.google.com/site/historiadelpiscosour/verdadera-historia-del-pisco-sour |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150713164959/https://sites.google.com/site/historiadelpiscosour/verdadera-historia-del-pisco-sour |archive-date=13 July 2015 |access-date=3 July 2015 |website=Revista Cultural de Lima |pages=72–73 |language=es}}</ref> Later, in 1924, with the aid of Morris' friend Nelson Rounsevell, the bar advertised its locale and invention in [[Valparaíso]], Chile. The advertisement featured in the Valparaíso newspaper ''South Pacific Mail'', owned by Rounsevell.<ref name=Toro /> By 1927, Morris' Bar had attained widespread notability for its cocktails, particularly the pisco sour. Brad Thomas Parsons writes that "the registry at the Morris Bar was filled with high praise from visitors who raved about the signature drink."<ref name=Tatiana />{{sfn|Parsons|2011|p=143}}

Notable attendees of Morris' Bar included the writers [[Abraham Valdelomar]] and [[José María Eguren]], the adventurers [[Richard Halliburton]] and [[Dean Ivan Lamb]], the anthropologist [[A. L. Kroeber|Alfred Kroeber]], and the businessmen [[Faucett Perú|Elmer Faucett]] and [[Corporación Lindley S.A.|José Lindley]].<ref name=Baird /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Toro-Lira |first=Guillermo L. |date=25 April 2020 |title=Análisis del registro de firmas del Morris' Bar (1916–1929) |url=https://piscopunch.com/analisis-del-registro-de-firmas-del-morris-bar-1916-1929/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210205152142/https://piscopunch.com/analisis-del-registro-de-firmas-del-morris-bar-1916-1929/ |archive-date=5 February 2021 |access-date=20 June 2021 |publisher=Piscopunch.com |language=es}}</ref> In his memoir, Lamb recalls his experience with the pisco sour in Morris' Bar, commenting that it "tasted like a pleasant soft drink" and that he [[Alcohol intoxication|felt disoriented]] after drinking a second one despite a bartender's stern objection that "one was usually sufficient."<ref name=Baird />

=== Spread ===

Over time, competition from nearby bars and Victor Morris' deteriorating health led to the decline and fall of his enterprise. Due to his worsening constitution, Morris delegated most of the bartending to his employees. Adding to the problem, nearby competitors, such as the [[Hotel Bolívar]] and the [[Country Club Lima Hotel]], housed bars that took clientele away from Morris' Bar. Moreover, Toro-Lira discovered that Morris accused four of his former bartenders of intellectual property theft after they left to work in one of these competing establishments.<ref name=Toro/> In 1929, Morris declared voluntary [[bankruptcy]] and closed his saloon. A few months later, on June 11, Victor Vaughen Morris died of [[cirrhosis]].<ref name=Tatiana/><ref name=Toro/>

[[File:2010-0109-Lima-HotelBolivar.jpg|thumb|right| alt=Photograph of a white five-story building |The [[Hotel Bolívar]] is among several Lima hotels still serving the ''pisco sour'' cocktail since the closure of Morris' Bar]]

Historian [[Luis Alberto Sánchez]] writes that, after Morris closed his bar, some of his bartenders left to work in other locales.<ref name=Tatiana/> Bruiget began working as a bartender for the nearby [[Grand Hotel Maury]], where he continued to serve his pisco sour recipe. His success with the drink led local Limean oral tradition to associate the Hotel Maury as the original home of the pisco sour.<ref name=Tatiana/> Sánchez, who in his youth also frequented Morris' Bar, writes in his memoir that two of Morris' other apprentices, Leonidas Cisneros Arteta and Augusto Rodríguez, opened their own bars.{{sfn|Sánchez|1969|p=249}}

As other former apprentices of Morris found work elsewhere, they also spread the pisco sour recipe.<ref name=Coloma/> Since at least 1927, pisco sours started being sold in Chile, most notably at the [[Club de la Unión (Chile)|Club de la Unión]], a high-class [[gentlemen's club]] in downtown [[Santiago]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Toro-Lira |first=Guillermo L. |date=6 February 2009 |title=La vida y pasiones de Víctor V. Morris, creador del Pisco Sour – 2nda parte |url=https://piscopunch.com/la-vida-y-pasiones-de-victor-v-morris-creador-del-pisco-sour-2nda-parte/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210516231325/https://piscopunch.com/la-vida-y-pasiones-de-victor-v-morris-creador-del-pisco-sour-2nda-parte/ |archive-date=16 May 2021 |access-date=16 May 2021 |publisher=Piscopunch.com |language=es}}</ref> During the 1930s, the drink made its way into [[California]], reaching bars as far north as the city of San Francisco.{{sfn|Kosmas|Zaric|2010|p=115}} Restaurateur [[Trader Vic's|Victor Jules Bergeron, Jr.]], remembers serving pisco sours at the original Trader Vic's [[tiki bar]] in [[Oakland, California|Oakland]], in 1934, to a traveler who had read about the cocktail in ''[[Life (magazine)|Life]]'' magazine.{{sfn|Bergeron|1972|p=398}} By at least the late 1960s, the cocktail also found its way to New York.{{sfn|Regan|2003|p=317}}

Beatriz Jiménez, a journalist from the Spanish newspaper ''[[El Mundo (Spain)|El Mundo]]'', indicates that back in Peru, the luxury hotels of Lima adopted the pisco sour as their own in the 1940s.<ref name="ElMundo">{{Cite web |last=Jiménez |first=Beatriz |date=6 February 2011 |title=La Fiesta del Pisco Sour |url=http://www.elmundo.es/america/2011/02/06/noticias/1297030740.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150703230216/http://www.elmundo.es/america/2011/02/06/noticias/1297030740.html |archive-date=3 July 2015 |access-date=2 July 2015 |website=El Mundo |location=Spain |language=es}}</ref> An oil bonanza attracted foreign attention to Peru during the 1940s and 1950s. In his 1943 [[Guide book|guidebook]] promoting "inter-American understanding" during the [[World War II|Second World War]], explorer Earl Parker Hanson writes that pisco and "the famous pisco sour" were favored by foreigners residing in Peru.{{sfn|Hanson|1943|p=34}} Among the foreign visitors to Lima were renowned [[Cinema of the United States|Hollywood]] actors who were fascinated by the pisco sour.<ref name=Emol/><ref>{{Cite web |last=Slater |first=Julia |date=9 February 2010 |title=Peru Toasts Pisco Boom on Annual Cocktail Day |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/expat/expatnews/7195368/Peru-toasts-pisco-boom-on-annual-cocktail-day.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170926041900/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/expat/expatnews/7195368/Peru-toasts-pisco-boom-on-annual-cocktail-day.html |archive-date=26 September 2017 |access-date=2 July 2015 |website=The Daily Telegraph}}</ref> Jiménez recollected oral traditions claiming an inebriated [[Ava Gardner]] had to be carried away by [[John Wayne]] after drinking too many pisco sours. [[Ernest Hemingway]] and [[Orson Welles]] are said to have been big fans of what they described as "that Peruvian drink."<ref name=ElMundo/> In his autobiography, actor [[Ray Milland]] recalls drinking the cocktail in Lima's [[Government Palace (Peru)|Government Palace]] during the [[José Luis Bustamante y Rivero|Bustamante presidency]] of the 1940s, first finding it "a most intriguing drink" and then, after delivering a "brilliant dedication speech" whose success he partly attributed to the cocktail, charmingly referred to it as "the lovely pisco sour."{{sfn|Milland|1974|p=207}}

In 1969, Sánchez wrote that the Hotel Maury still served the "authentic" Pisco sour from Morris' Bar.{{sfn|Sánchez|1969|p=249}}  [[Pan Am|Pan American World Airways]] included the pisco sour in a drinking tips section for the 1978 edition of its ''Encyclopedia of Travel'' guidebook, warning travelers to Peru that "[t]he pisco sour looks innocent, but is potent."{{sfn|Pan American World Airways, Inc.|1978|p=862}} Bolivian journalist Ted Córdova Claure wrote, in 1984, that the Hotel Bolívar stood as a monument to the decadence of the Peruvian oligarchy (in Spanish: "''Este hotel es un monumento a la decadencia de la oligarquía peruana.''"). He noted the locale as being the traditional home of the pisco sour and recommended it as one of the best hotels in Lima.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Córdova Claure |first=Ted |year=1984 |title=La Calcutización de las Ciudades Latinoamericanas |url=http://www.nuso.org/upload/articulos/1228_1.pdf |url-status=dead |journal=Nueva Sociedad |language=es |issue=72 |pages=49–56 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100706185507/http://www.nuso.org/upload/articulos/1228_1.pdf |archive-date=2010-07-06 |access-date=2012-03-14}}</ref> Nowadays, the Hotel Bolivar continues to offer the cocktail in its "El Bolivarcito" bar,<ref>{{Cite web |title=El Bolivarcito |url=http://www.granhotelbolivar.com.pe/ingles/home.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150708155754/http://www.granhotelbolivar.com.pe/ingles/home.html |archive-date=8 July 2015 |access-date=2 July 2015 |publisher=GranHotelBolivar.com}}</ref> while the Country Club Lima Hotel offers the drink in its "English Bar" saloon.<ref>{{Cite web |year=2013 |title=English Bar |url=http://www.hotelcountry.com/dinning/english-bar/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150703191810/http://www.hotelcountry.com/dinning/english-bar/ |archive-date=3 July 2015 |access-date=2 July 2015 |publisher=HotelCountry.com}}</ref>

== Preparation and variants ==
[[File:El que trae aguardiente de Yca. 1820).jpg|thumb | left|upright| alt=Painting of a man on horseback leading two pack animals |Pisco transported in [[earthenware]] containers of the same name from [[Ica, Peru]]. [[Watercolor painting]] by [[Afro-Peruvian]] artist [[Pancho Fierro]]]]

The pisco sour has three different methods of preparation. The Peruvian pisco sour cocktail is made by mixing Peruvian [[pisco]] with [[Key lime]] juice, [[simple syrup]], [[egg white]], [[Angostura bitters]] (for [[Cocktail garnish|garnish]]), and ice cubes.{{sfn|Kosmas|Zaric|2010|p=115}} The Chilean pisco sour cocktail is made by mixing Chilean Pisco with [[limón de Pica]] juice, [[powdered sugar]], and ice cubes.{{sfn|Castillo-Feliú|2000|p=79}} Daniel Joelson, a food writer, and critic, contends that the major difference between both pisco sour versions "is that Peruvians generally include egg whites, while Chileans do not."<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Joelson |first=Daniel |date=Winter 2004 |title=The Pisco Wars |journal=Gastronomica: The Journal of Food and Culture |volume=4 |issue=1 |pages=6–8 |doi=10.1525/gfc.2004.4.1.1}}</ref> The version from the [[International Bartenders Association]], which lists the pisco sour among its "[[List of IBA official cocktails|New Era Drinks]]," is similar to the Peruvian version, but with the difference that it uses [[lemon]] juice, instead of lime juice, and does not distinguish between the two different types of pisco.<ref>{{Cite web |title=New Era Drinks |url=http://iba-world.com/new-era-drinks/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20151028231612/http://iba-world.com/new-era-drinks/ |archive-date=28 October 2015 |access-date=28 October 2015 |website=IBA-World.com |publisher=International Bartenders Association}}</ref>

Considerable differences exist in the pisco used in the cocktails. According to food and wine expert Mark Spivak, the difference is in how both beverages are produced; whereas "Chilean pisco is mass-produced," the Peruvian version "is made in small batches."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Spivak |first=Mark |title=Pour&nbsp;– Pisco Fever |url=http://www.palmbeachillustrated.com/index.cfm?fuseaction=news.details&ArticleId=1609& |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150518070453/http://www.palmbeachillustrated.com/index.cfm?fuseaction=news.details&ArticleId=1609& |archive-date=18 May 2015 |access-date=3 July 2015 |publisher=PalmBeachIllustrated.com}}</ref> Cocktail historian Andrew Bohrer focuses his comparison on taste, claiming that "[i]n Peru, pisco is made in a pot still, distilled to proof, and un-aged; it is very similar to [[grappa]]. In Chile, pisco is made in a column still and aged in wood; it is similar to a very light [[cognac]]."{{sfn|Bohrer|2012|loc=Friendship Test}} Chilean [[Oenology|oenologist]] Patricio Tapia adds that while Chilean pisco producers usually mix vine stocks, Peruvian producers have specific pisco types that use the [[aroma of wine|aromatic]] qualities of vines such as [[Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains|Yellow Muscat]] and [[Italia (grape)|Italia]]. Tapia concludes this is why Peruvian pisco bottles denote their [[vintage]] year and the Chilean versions do not.<ref name="Tapia">{{Cite web |year=2012 |title=Entrevista: Patricio Tapia – Periodista Chileno Especializado en Vinos |url=http://piscosour.com/2013/01/entrevista-patricio-tapia-periodista-chileno-especializado-en-vinos/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131212195354/http://piscosour.com/2013/01/entrevista-patricio-tapia-periodista-chileno-especializado-en-vinos/ |archive-date=12 December 2013 |access-date=3 July 2015 |publisher=PiscoSour.com |language=es}}</ref>

Variations of the pisco sour exist in Peru, [[Bolivia]], and Chile. There are adaptations of the cocktail in Peru using fruits such as ''[[Passiflora edulis|maracuya]]'' (commonly known as passion fruit), ''[[Physalis peruviana|aguaymanto]]'', and apples, or traditional ingredients such as the [[coca]] leaf.<ref>{{Cite web |year=2012 |title=Recetas |url=http://piscosour.com/?cat=3 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120826225947/http://piscosour.com/?cat=3 |archive-date=2012-08-26 |access-date=2012-12-03 |publisher=PiscoSour.com |language=es}}</ref> Lima's Hotel Bolivar serves a larger version of the cocktail, named ''pisco sour catedral'', invented for hurried guests arriving from the nearby [[Cathedral Basilica of Lima|Catholic cathedral]].<ref>{{Cite web |date= |title=Pisco sour catedral y el Gran Hotel Bolívar |url=https://elpisco.es/pisco-sour-catedral/ |access-date=2021-06-22 |publisher=Elpisco.es |language=es |archive-date=2021-06-24 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210624205821/https://elpisco.es/pisco-sour-catedral/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> In Chile, variants include the ají Sour (with a spicy green chili), mango sour (with [[mango]] juice), and sour de campo (with ginger and honey). In Bolivia, the [[Yunqueño]] variant (from its [[Bolivian Yungas|Yungas region]]) replaces the lime with orange juice.{{sfn|Baez Kijac|2003|p=35}}

Other cocktails containing pisco exist in Chile and Peru. The Chilean [[piscola]] is made by mixing pisco with [[cola]].{{sfn|Castillo-Feliú|2000|p=79}} The [[Algarrobina cocktail]], popular in northern Peru, is made from pisco, [[condensed milk]], and sap from the Peruvian ''[[Prosopis humilis|algarroba]]'' tree.{{sfn|Albala|2011|p=266}} Other Peruvian pisco-based cocktails include the ''chilcano'' (made with pisco and [[ginger ale]]) and the ''capitán'' (made with pisco and [[vermouth]]).{{sfn|Pan American World Airways, Inc.|1978|p=862}} Another similar cocktail, from the United States, is the Californian pisco punch, originally made with Peruvian pisco, pineapples, and lemon.<ref>See:
* {{harvnb|Kosmas|Zaric|2010|p=116}}
* {{harvnb|Sandham|2012|p=251}}</ref>

== Popularity ==
[[File:Presidentes Cristina Fernandez y Alan Garcia brindan con pisco.jpg| thumb| right| alt=Photograph |Argentine president [[Cristina Fernández de Kirchner|Cristina Fernández]] and Peruvian president [[Alan García]] toast with a pisco sour at Peru's [[Government Palace (Peru)|Government Palace]] in 2010]]

Duggan McDonnell describes the pisco sour as "Latin America's most elegant cocktail, frothy, balanced, bright yet rich," adding that "Barkeeps throughout Northern California will attest that they have shaken many a Pisco sour. It is the egg white cocktail of choice and an absolutely beloved one by most."{{sfn|McDonnell|2015|p=170}} Australian journalist Kate Schneider writes that the pisco sour "has become so famous that there is an International Pisco Sour Day celebration on the first Saturday in February every year, as well as a [[Facebook]] page with more than 600,000 likes."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Schneider |first=Kate |date=March 27, 2012 |title=Kate Schneider Appreciates the Bitter Side of Life with South American Drink Pisco Sour |url=http://www.news.com.au/travel/world-travel/raise-a-glass-to-souring-your-trip/story-e6frfqai-1226311153834 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150623103659/http://www.news.com.au/travel/world-travel/raise-a-glass-to-souring-your-trip/story-e6frfqai-1226311153834 |archive-date=23 June 2015 |access-date=2 July 2015 |publisher=News.com.au}}</ref> According to Chilean [[entrepreneur]] Rolando Hinrichs Oyarce, owner of a restaurant-bar in [[Spain]], "The pisco sour is highly international, just like [[Cebiche]], and so they are not too unknown" (Spanish: "''El pisco sour es bastante internacional, al igual que el cebiche, por lo tanto no son tan desconocidos''").<ref>{{Cite news |last=Alías |first=Marina |date=April 9, 2006 |title=Sours chilenos irrumpen en barrio mítico de Madrid |language=es |work=El Mercurio, Revista del Domingo |location=Santiago}}</ref> In 2003, Peru created the "''Día Nacional del Pisco Sour''" (National Pisco Sour Day), an official government holiday celebrated on the first Saturday of February.<ref name="Comercio">{{Cite web |date=8 February 2011 |title=Chile Celebra Hoy el Día de la Piscola |url=http://elcomercio.pe/gastronomia/bares-y-copas/chile-tambien-tiene-su-bebida-nacional-hoy-celebran-dia-piscola-noticia-710673 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150703214000/http://elcomercio.pe/gastronomia/bares-y-copas/chile-tambien-tiene-su-bebida-nacional-hoy-celebran-dia-piscola-noticia-710673 |archive-date=3 July 2015 |access-date=3 July 2015 |website=El Comercio |publisher=Empresa Editora El Comercio |language=es}}</ref>{{efn-ua|The "''Día Nacional del Pisco Sour''" holiday was initially set for celebration on February 8.<ref name="Comercio" /> However, after the Chilean Pisco industry set its non-government sponsored "''Día de la Piscola''" (Piscola Day) also for celebration on February 8,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ruiz |first=Carlos |date=8 February 2011 |title=Hoy es el Día de la Piscola: Chilenos Celebran Uno de dus Tragos Típicos |url=http://www.elobservatodo.cl/admin/render/noticia/19531 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150923235939/http://www.elobservatodo.cl/admin/render/noticia/19531 |archive-date=23 September 2015 |access-date=3 July 2015 |publisher=ElObservatodo.cl |language=es}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Castro |first=Felipe |date=8 February 2011 |title=Día de la Piscola: A Tomar Combinados |url=http://www.lanacion.cl/dia-de-la-piscola-a-tomar-combinados/noticias/2011-02-07/191611.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150704105230/http://www.lanacion.cl/dia-de-la-piscola-a-tomar-combinados/noticias/2011-02-07/191611.html |archive-date=4 July 2015 |access-date=3 July 2015 |publisher=LaNacion.cl |language=es}}</ref> Peru responded by changing its pisco sour holiday to its current date.<ref name=Comercio />}} During the 2008 [[Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation|APEC]] Economic Leaders' Meeting, Peru promoted its pisco sour with widespread acceptance. The cocktail was reportedly the preferred drink of the attendees, mostly leaders, businessmen, and delegates.<ref>{{Cite web |date=24 November 2008 |title=APEC visitors enjoyed Peruvian pisco sour |url=http://www.andina.com.pe/agencia/noticia-apec-visitors-enjoyed-peruvian-pisco-sour-205750.aspx |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150703041827/http://www.andina.com.pe/agencia/noticia-apec-visitors-enjoyed-peruvian-pisco-sour-205750.aspx |archive-date=3 July 2015 |access-date=2 July 2015 |publisher=Andina.com}}</ref>

=== Origin dispute ===
Victor Vaughen Morris is considered by most historians to be the inventor of the pisco sour cocktail.{{sfn|Duecy|2013|loc=Pisco Sour}} Nonetheless, the cocktail's traditional origin story is complicated with findings that suggest otherwise. Based on the recipe from the 1903 Peruvian cookbook ''Manual de Cocina a la Criolla'', researcher Nico Vera considers that "the origin of the Pisco Sour may be a traditional [[Criollo people|creole]] cocktail made in Lima over 100 years ago."<ref name="PeruvianTimes">{{Cite web |last=Vecchio |first=Rick |date=23 January 2014 |title=Beguiling History of the Pisco Sour, With a Twist |url=https://www.peruviantimes.com/23/beguiling-history-of-the-pisco-sour-with-a-twist/21309/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210614022828/https://www.peruviantimes.com/23/beguiling-history-of-the-pisco-sour-with-a-twist/21309/ |archive-date=14 June 2021 |access-date=18 June 2021 |website=Peruvian Times |publisher=Andean Air Mail & Peruvian Times}}</ref> Based on the clipping from the 1921 ''West Coast Leader'' news article, McDonnell considers it possible that the pisco sour may have actually originated in San Francisco, considering additionally that during this time the city experienced a "burst of cocktail creativity," the [[whiskey sour]] cocktail "was plentiful and ubiquitous," and "the fact that Pisco was heralded as a special spirit" in the city.{{sfn|McDonnell|2015|p=171}}

[[File:Wong Event.jpg|thumb|upright|left|alt=Photograph of two persons in front of a sculpture|Morris was honored, in 2009, with a bust in Peru]]

In defense of Morris, journalist Rick Vecchio considers that "even if there was something very similar and pre-existing" to Morris' pisco cocktail, it should not be doubted that he "was the first to serve, promote and perfect what today is known as the Pisco Sour."<ref name=PeruvianTimes /> McDonnell also considers that, regardless of its exact origin, the pisco sour "belongs to Peru."{{sfn|McDonnell|2015|p=172}} According to culture writer Saxon Baird, a [[Bust (sculpture)|bust]] in honor of Morris stands in Lima's [[Santiago de Surco]] district "as a testament to Morris' contribution to modern Peruvian culture and the country he called home for more than half his life."<ref name=Baird />

Despite this, an ongoing dispute exists between Chile and Peru over the origin of the pisco sour.{{sfn|Sandham|2012|p=251}} In Chile, a local [[Urban legend|story]] developed in the 1980s attributing the invention of the pisco sour to Elliot Stubb, an English steward from a [[sailing ship]] named ''Sunshine''. Chilean folklorist and historian [[Oreste Plath]] contributed to the legend's propagation by writing that, according to the Peruvian newspaper ''El Comercio de Iquique'', in 1872, after obtaining leave to disembark, Stubb opened a bar in the then-Peruvian port of [[Iquique]] and invented the pisco sour while experimenting with drinks.<ref name=Toro />{{sfn|Plath|1981|p=106}}{{efn-ua|Iquique was later occupied by Chile during the [[War of the Pacific]] and annexed by that country in 1883.}} Nevertheless, researcher Toro-Lira argues that the story was refuted after it was discovered ''El Comercio de Iquique'' was actually referring to the invention of the whiskey sour.<ref name=Toro /> The story of Elliot Stubb and his alleged invention of the whiskey sour in Iquique is also found in a 1962 publication by the [[National University of Cuyo|University of Cuyo]], [[Argentina]]. An excerpt from the newspaper's story has Elliot Stubb stating, "From now on&nbsp;... this shall be my drink of battle, my favorite drink, and it shall be named Whisky Sour" (in Spanish: "''En adelante dijo Elliot — éste será mi trago de batalla, — mi trago favorito —, y se llamará Whisky Sour.''").{{sfn|Facultad de Filosofía y Letras|1962|p=[https://books.google.com/books?hl=es&q=En%20adelante%20dijo%20Elliot%20%E2%80%94%20%C3%A9ste%20ser%C3%A1%20mi%20trago%20de%20batalla%2C%20%E2%80%94%20mi%20trago%20favorito%20%E2%80%94%20%2C%20y%20se%20llamar%C3%A1%20Whisky%20Sour%20(sour%2C%20el%20%C3%A1cido%20del%20lim%C3%B3n)&sourceid=navclient-ff&rlz=1B5GGGL_esPE318&um=1&ie=UTF-8&sa=N&tab=wp 385]}}

Some pisco producers have expressed that the ongoing controversy between Chile and Peru helps promote interest in the liquor and its [[geographical indication]] dispute.<ref>{{Cite web |date=30 May 2017 |title=Nuevo 'round' en la larga pelea de Perú y Chile por el pisco |url=https://cnnespanol.cnn.com/2017/05/30/nuevo-round-en-la-larga-pelea-de-peru-y-chile-por-el-pisco/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210515164552/https://cnnespanol.cnn.com/2017/05/30/nuevo-round-en-la-larga-pelea-de-peru-y-chile-por-el-pisco/ |archive-date=15 May 2021 |access-date=15 May 2021 |website=CNN en Español |publisher=CNN |language=es}}</ref>

American [[celebrity chef]] [[Anthony Bourdain]] drew attention to the cocktail when, in an episode of his [[Travel Channel]] program ''[[Anthony Bourdain: No Reservations|No Reservations]]'', he drank a pisco sour in Valparaíso, Chile, and said: "that's good, but ... next time, I'll have a beer." The broadcaster [[Radio Programas del Perú]] reported that Jorge López Sotomayor, the episode's Chilean producer and Bourdain's travel partner in Chile, said Bourdain found the pisco sour he drank in Valparaíso boring and not worth the effort (in Spanish: "''A mí me dijo que el pisco sour lo encontró aburrido y que no valía la pena.''"). Lopez added that Bourdain had recently arrived from Peru, where he drank several pisco sours which he thought tasted better than the Chilean version.<ref>{{Cite news |date=18 July 2009 |title=Chef Anthony Bourdain: Pisco Sour Chileno es Aburrido y No Vale la Pena |language=es |work=Radio Programas del Perú |publisher=Grupo RPP |url=http://www.rpp.com.pe/2009-07-18-chef-anthony-bourdain-pisco-sour-chileno-es-aburrido-y-no-vale-la-pena-noticia_195507.html |url-status=live |access-date=2 July 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150924092350/http://www.rpp.com.pe/2009-07-18-chef-anthony-bourdain-pisco-sour-chileno-es-aburrido-y-no-vale-la-pena-noticia_195507.html |archive-date=24 September 2015}}</ref>

In 2010, Mexican singer-songwriter [[Aleks Syntek]] humorously posted on [[Twitter]] that the pisco sour is Chilean and, after receiving critical responses to his statement, apologized and mentioned he was only joking.<ref>{{Cite web |date=19 November 2010 |title=Alex Syntek Dice Que el pisco sour y La Tigresa del Oriente Son Chilenos |url=http://www.rpp.com.pe/detalle.php?nid=312238&op=7 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101124075613/http://www.rpp.com.pe/detalle.php?nid=312238&op=7 |archive-date=24 November 2010 |access-date=2 July 2015 |website=Radio Programas del Perú |publisher=Grupo RPP |language=es}}</ref> Mexican television host and comedian [[Adal Ramones]] also joked about pisco sour, about the [[2009 Chile–Peru espionage scandal]], on November 17, 2009. Ramones, a fan of Peruvian Pisco, when asked about the espionage, asked what Chileans were spying on in Peru, suggesting it might be how to make a pisco sour (in Spanish: "''¿Qué quieren espiar los chilenos? ¿Cómo hacer pisco sour?''").<ref>{{Cite web |date=17 November 2009 |title=Adal Ramones: "¿Qué Quieren Espiar los Chilenos? ¿Cómo Hacer Pisco Sour?" |url=http://elcomercio.pe/tvmas/television/adal-ramones-que-quieren-espiar-chilenos-como-se-hace-buen-pisco-sour-noticia-370244 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150703013118/http://elcomercio.pe/tvmas/television/adal-ramones-que-quieren-espiar-chilenos-como-se-hace-buen-pisco-sour-noticia-370244 |archive-date=3 July 2015 |access-date=2 July 2015 |website=El Comercio |publisher=Empresa Editora El Comercio |language=es}}</ref> In 2017, when told pisco sour was "totally Chilean" by an interviewer at a Chilean radio station, British musician [[Ed Sheeran]] commented that he preferred the Peruvian pisco sour.<ref>{{Cite web |date=19 May 2017 |title=Ed Sheeran cree que el pisco peruano es mejor que el chileno… ¿y tú? |url=https://cnnespanol.cnn.com/2017/05/19/a-ed-sheeran-cree-que-el-pisco-peruano-es-mejor-que-el-chileno-y-a-ti/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210515164552/https://cnnespanol.cnn.com/2017/05/19/a-ed-sheeran-cree-que-el-pisco-peruano-es-mejor-que-el-chileno-y-a-ti/ |archive-date=15 May 2021 |access-date=15 May 2021 |website=CNN en Español |publisher=CNN |language=es}}</ref>

